# MRL family 10 (SPECIFICALLY OWED): (a{1,3}){65} against subjects of
# 65..100 'a' characters.
#
# *** FINDING -- RESOLVED 2026-08-17 by the build lane, kept in place
# *** rather than edited away because it is the reason this file exists.
# *** WHAT THE BLINDED AUTHOR FOUND, verbatim below, and what happened to
# *** it: the gap was real and their diagnosis was right. On the counter
# *** rung ONE emitted body copy serves every trip, so the compiler's
# *** compile-time view of "how many mandatory iterations still follow"
# *** tops out at K + residue -- 9 here, where the truth is 65 -- and the
# *** bound never bit. k23_design.md S4.5 had DESIGNED the runtime form
# *** (read the count out of the trailed counter slot) and the build lane
# *** had deferred it as a residual on S9.3's "once-per-trip is believed
# *** enough"; that split into a FREQUENCY which holds and a VALUE which
# *** does not. Building the runtime term makes every case below pass and
# *** answers N=65 in 0.13 s. Nothing in this file was weakened.
# *** THE EXPECTATIONS WERE INDEPENDENTLY RE-CONFIRMED (build lane, python
# *** re over 105 (reps, N) cells at reps 5-15, 0 mismatches): the closed
# *** form below is exact, and its "last repetition is one byte" clause
# *** holds while N - 65 <= 2*64 = 128, i.e. N <= 193 -- comfortably
# *** outside the N <= 100 this file pins. The clause is NOT unconditional:
# *** at N = 3*reps - 1 the last repetition takes 2, which is where a
# *** careless restatement of the rule goes wrong.
# ***
# *** THE ORIGINAL FINDING, as written when it was live:
# *** every 'm'/'g' case in this file FAILS against the pcrec
# *** binary under test -- not because the expectations below are wrong
# *** (they check out, see "THE REASONING" and the cross-checks below,
# *** and were independently confirmed correct once the matcher was
# *** allowed to actually finish -- see the report), but because the
# *** matcher GIVES UP: it exhausts the VM's step budget (exit 3, "steps")
# *** instead of answering. This is a direct violation of the "FASTER"
# *** half of the MRL promise for the precise case the project owed.
# *** Root-caused (see the report): with --no-captures the identical
# *** pattern answers instantly and correctly, so MRL pruning DOES work
# *** on the DFA/no-captures path; on the VM/captures path (today's
# *** default -- what these 'g' lines require), the same pattern with
# *** --fno-step-budget (no cap) took 1.2s at 32 repetitions and did not
# *** finish within 20s at 65 (this file's actual case). The promise's
# *** OWN motivating shape, (a{10,20}){10,50}, answers instantly WITH
# *** captures on (verified directly, and see tests/mrl/11's small-scale
# *** echo of that shape, which passes cleanly) -- so this is not a
# *** blanket VM/capture failure, it is specific to an EXACT-count (or
# *** narrow-range) outer quantifier over a NARROW inner range at a
# *** total repeat count upward of roughly 32 (measured boundary: 31
# *** repetitions of (a{1,3}) or (a{2,3}) answers instantly, 32 does not,
# *** regardless of how much slack the subject has above the minimum).
# *** DO NOT weaken these expectations to make the test pass -- they are
# *** the correct answers; the matcher's failure to reach them is the bug.
#
# python's `re` module does NOT terminate on this pattern within any
# reasonable timeout (confirmed: 15s timeout hit at both N=65 and N=100)
# -- this is exactly the ambiguous-decomposition region the brief warns
# about. Every expectation below is derived from a closed-form argument
# about PCRE's leftmost-greedy backtracking semantics (reasoning and its
# own verification recorded in the generator script that produced this
# file, tests/mrl/../gen_rxt.py is NOT checked in here since it's outside
# tests/mrl/ -- the reasoning is repeated in full below so this file
# stands alone).
#
# THE REASONING, restated:
#   - The outer quantifier is EXACT ({65}, min=max=65): the match must
#     complete exactly 65 repetitions of the inner a{1,3}, no more, no
#     fewer. There is no "outer wants more reps" ambiguity the way a
#     RANGE outer quantifier has (contrast the promise's own motivating
#     case, (a{10,20}){10,50}, whose outer IS a range).
#   - Nothing follows the group in this pattern, so a completed 65-rep
#     match succeeds immediately -- there's no requirement to consume the
#     whole subject, only a requirement that all 65 reps individually
#     succeed (each needs at least 1 byte).
#   - Standard leftmost-greedy backtracking tries the largest inner count
#     (3) at each repetition first, backtracking to the most recent
#     repetition with an untried smaller alternative whenever a LATER
#     repetition can't reach its own minimum (1) because bytes ran out.
#   - This converges (proof + empirical cross-check at reps up to 20:
#     see the generator) to: greedily take 3 at each repetition AS LONG
#     AS enough bytes remain for every later repetition's minimum (1
#     each); once that slack is exhausted, every remaining repetition
#     (including all the way through repetition 65) is forced to exactly
#     1. Since N is always within [65,100] and the minimum viable slack
#     absorption is 2 extra bytes per repetition (3-1), for N<=100 the
#     slack (N-65, at most 35) is fully soaked up within the first ~18
#     repetitions, leaving the LAST repetition -- and therefore the
#     capture group's span -- always exactly 1 byte wide, for every N in
#     this range.
#   - CONSEQUENCE, for every N in [65,100]: the overall match consumes
#     the ENTIRE subject (span [0,N)), and capture group 1 (the last
#     completed iteration) is the single final byte, span [N-1,N).
#   - Below N=65 (the outer's own minimum requirement), no match is
#     possible at any start position -- this direction IS fast for a
#     real backtracker to discover (verified: python's re on the smaller-
#     reps cross-check returns instantly for N below the total minimum,
#     since the shortfall is detected without exponential exploration),
#     so the 'n' cases below ARE python-verified, unlike the 'm'/'g' ones.
#
# CONFIDENCE: high for all N in [65,100] pinned below (closed form
# verified against literal DFS backtracking and python re for reps up to
# 20, agreeing in all 132+ cross-checked combinations including the most
# degenerate N=reps point). The one thing NOT directly verified at
# reps=65 itself is pcrec's C implementation matching textbook greedy
# backtracking exactly at this specific depth -- stated once here as the
# residual caveat, not per-case.


# N=64: one byte short of the outer's own minimum (65 mandatory reps, >=1 byte each) -- no match anywhere. This direction IS fast for python re to confirm.
pattern (a{1,3}){65}
n "aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa"

# N=0: the empty subject, far below the minimum.
pattern (a{1,3}){65}
n ""

# N=65: 0 bytes of slack over the 65-rep minimum, front-loaded into the earliest reps; last rep forced to 1.
pattern (a{1,3}){65}
m "aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa" 0 65
g 1 64 65

# N=66: 1 bytes of slack over the 65-rep minimum, front-loaded into the earliest reps; last rep forced to 1.
pattern (a{1,3}){65}
m "aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa" 0 66
g 1 65 66

# N=67: 2 bytes of slack over the 65-rep minimum, front-loaded into the earliest reps; last rep forced to 1.
pattern (a{1,3}){65}
m "aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa" 0 67
g 1 66 67

